    fail("indexing a " + typeof c)
    return undefined
}

// List element (negative wraps) or Map entry assignment.
// js_pyset is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs (five languages, knobs rtkIsDict and
// rtkDictFind; the index coercion now truncates, which is the oracle).

// js_dtidx / js_dtidxset are DART's list and string indexing, emitted by
// dart-to-llvm-ir.abnf in place of js_pyget / js_pyset. The shared pair is PYTHON's,
// and routing Dart through it made ONE out-of-range index answer three different
// things (docs/todo.md 1.6): this half ABORTED with "list index out of range: 7", the
// Go twin raised python's IndexError, and the interpreter aborted with a third
// wording. --cross could never see it - it diffs a language's two HALVES, and two of
// the three legs are the same half.
//
// dart:core's List.operator [] is documented "Throws a RangeError if index is out of
// bounds"; the object thrown is an IndexError (errors.dart:445, `class IndexError
// extends ArgumentError implements RangeError`). Its toString comes out of the
// dtExcArgStr / dtExcExpl pair below:
//
//	RangeError (index): Index out of range: index should be less than 3: 7
//
// The "(index)" is the error's `name` and is passed EXPLICITLY here rather than
// defaulted inside dtExcArgNew - see the long note at js_dtidx in abnf/jsrtdart.go
// for why, and for what that leaves inexact. Python's negative-index WRAP goes with
// it: `xs[-1]` is a RangeError in Dart, not the last element.
function dtIndexErr(k, c) {
    throw dtExcNew("IndexError", "", [k, c, "index"])
